A unit interpolated bare renders as its magnitude per base, on every target.

`"{d}"` of a 500ms duration is `500ms` everywhere; `"{m}"` of `3px + 2em` is
`3px + 2em` everywhere. The rule falls out of the representation rather than
being a second decision on top of it: a unit value IS a magnitude per base, so
what it displays as is those magnitudes, each with the base's own suffix,
joined the way the source would have written the sum.

Before this, one program had five answers and which one a program got
depended on whether the value happened to const-fold. A var-held 500ms was
`500ms` on Go (time.Duration's own String), `500` on JavaScript (String of a
number), `500.0` on Kotlin (Double.toString) and `500ms` on the interpreter --
and a multi-base value was worse, because nobody had chosen those spellings at
all: Go printed the raw struct `{3 2 0 0 0}`, JavaScript `[object Object]` and
Kotlin `Measurement(px=3.0, em=2.0, ...)`. A const-folded one was `500` on all
four, because the optimizer folded a unit to the bare float64 its magnitude is
and spelled that back with `%v`.

Go's apparent agreement with the interpreter was a coincidence of the value
1100ms prints as `1.1s` there, because time.Duration's String normalises
across units while the declared base is ms. So the one cell of the table that
looked right was right for 500 and wrong for 1100, which is why the fixture
carries both.

The written suffix is not consulted: `2rem` displays as `32em`. A compiled
target holds the record and nothing else, so a spelling to prefer is a memory
only the interpreter had. `0rem` and `0px` are the same value and print the
same, in the first declared base.

Go needs a helper per unit because it has no expression form for the join;
JavaScript and Kotlin inline an arrow and a `let`, which is also what binds
the operand so it is not re-evaluated once per base. The magnitude itself goes
through whatever each target already spells a bare float with, so unit display
inherits the float agreement rather than restating it -- Kotlin's
`_snglFloatStr` is there for that reason.

This is not the CSS path. A style prop is spelled by internal/htmlutil, from a
literal, with its own rename table (`pct` is written `%` there); it shares
nothing with this but ir.UnitMagnitude, and `7px` in a stylesheet is unchanged
by any of the above.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `fmt\.Sprint\(m\.mix__inst0\)` -- the raw struct print, which gave `{3 2 0 0 0}`
deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `mix__inst0\.toString\(\)` -- the data class print, which gave `Measurement(px=3.0, em=2.0, ...)`
deny out/none/html/index.html `String\(state\.mix__inst0\)` -- String of the per-base object, which gave `[object Object]`
deny * `fixed 250"` -- the const-folded spelling: the optimizer reduced a unit to its bare magnitude and spelled that back, so a const said `250` where the identical var said `250ms`

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:time"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

unit tick {
    tk
}

component main ui.node {
    // Two independent bases, so no single number and no single suffix.
    var mix ui.measurement = 3px + 2em
    // Reduces through `rem = 16em`, and displays in the base it counts in.
    var reduced ui.measurement = 2rem
    // One base, so the whole value is a number of tk.
    var ticks tick = 4tk
    // 500 is the value Go's own String happens to agree on; 1100 is the one
    // it does not, printing `1.1s` for a unit whose declared base is ms.
    var half time.duration = 500ms
    var over time.duration = 500ms + 600ms
    // A const folds before any backend sees it, which is a fifth answer
    // rather than one of the four.
    const fixed time.duration = 250ms

    ui.text(value="mix {mix}")
    ui.text(value="reduced {reduced}")
    ui.text(value="ticks {ticks}")
    ui.text(value="half {half}")
    ui.text(value="over {over}")
    ui.text(value="fixed {fixed}")
}

ui.window {
    main
}
